\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Inverse response for an invertible precision}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{Let #{M} be a unital module over a commutative unital ring #{R},
with a \vocabk{chosen perfect symmetric pairing}{fdip-001O} #{b}.
Let #{L:M\to M} be \vocabk{self-adjoint}{fdip-001P}, and let
#{\mu\in R}. The endomorphism
##{Q=L+\mu I_M:M\longrightarrow M}
is self-adjoint. Assume additionally that #{Q} is an
\vocabk{isomorphism}{ca-0015}, and put #{G=Q^{-1}}. Then
#{G^\dagger=G}, and for every source #{j\in M} the equation
##{Qf=j}
has the unique solution #{f=Gj}.}

\p{This is a direct consequence of the
[adjoint and inverse laws](fdip-001P) and the two-sided inverse
convention of \citet{Definition 4.2.4, Tag 0017}{stacks2017stacks}.}

\proof{
\p{Linearity of the adjoint and #{I^\dagger=I} give
#{Q^\dagger=L^\dagger+\mu I=Q}. The inverse-adjoint law gives
#{G^\dagger=(Q^\dagger)^{-1}=G}. Since #{QG=I}, #{Q(Gj)=j}.
Conversely, if #{Qf=j}, then #{GQ=I} gives #{f=Gj}.}
}

\p{For #{M=\mathcal F_R(V)} on a finite set with its
\vocabk{perfect counting duality}{fdip-001F}, define the point kernel
only after this inverse map exists:
##{G(v,u)=(G\delta_u)(v).}
The indicator expansion and linearity give
##{(Gf)(v)=\sum_{u\in V}G(v,u)f(u).}
Moreover,
##{\begin{aligned}
G(v,u)&=b(\delta_v,G\delta_u)\\
 &=b(G\delta_v,\delta_u)=G(u,v).
\end{aligned}}
Thus the symmetric array presents the already defined inverse.
On the zero module the unique endomorphism is the identity and is
invertible; for an empty carrier the kernel and these sums are empty.}

\p{A unit mass coefficient does not ensure invertibility over a ring.
Over #{\mathbb Z}, take one unit-weight edge #{0\to1}, the
[Laplacian](fdip-0007) #{L(a,b)=(a-b,b-a)}, and #{\mu=1}. Then
##{Q(a,b)=(2a-b,2b-a).}
If #{Q(a,b)=(1,0)}, the second equation gives #{a=2b}, and the first
gives #{3b=1}, impossible in #{\mathbb Z}. Thus #{Q} is not onto,
even though the mass and edge weight are units. The
[real massive Green operator](fdip-000A) acquires invertibility from
positive energy and finite dimension. No positivity, spectrum or
probability law is asserted over an arbitrary #{R}.}
